# Security engagement

This file is the engagement record for the `security-testing` bundle. It was
written by `evidence.mjs engagement init` (step 0) because no
`.agents/security-testing/engagement.md` existed. **Edit the JSON block below,
then run `engagement init` again.** Until you do, every command that needs the
record exits `2 ENGAGEMENT-MISSING` (file absent) or
`2 ENGAGEMENT-INVALID(<reason>)` (file present, block wrong).

The prose in this file is yours: keep notes, links, the people involved,
anything. The scripts read **only** the one fenced block tagged
`json engagement` — there must be exactly one, it must be strict JSON (no
comments, no trailing commas, no duplicate keys, integers only), and it must
validate against `security-evidence/references/engagement.schema.json`.
`run init` snapshots the parsed block into `<run>/engagement.json`, so a run
always carries the record it was started with.

## The record

```json engagement
{
  "engagement_id": "eng-2026-001",
  "slug": "my-product",
  "scope_paths": ["src/"],
  "product_paths": ["src/", "package.json", "package-lock.json"],
  "targets": {
    "tracker": ["github.com"],
    "browser": ["staging.example.com"],
    "repo": "my-org/my-product"
  },
  "base_url": "https://staging.example.com",
  "execute_project_tests": {
    "argv": ["npm", "test"],
    "timeout_s": 600,
    "install": {
      "argv": ["npm", "ci"],
      "allow_tracked_changes": false
    }
  },
  "sign_off": {
    "require_dispositions": "executed-or-ticketed"
  },
  "artifact_policy": {
    "ledger": "local",
    "runs": "local",
    "receipts": "local",
    "proposals": "local",
    "handoffs": "local",
    "imports": "local",
    "register": "local",
    "reports": "local",
    "cases": "local"
  }
}
```

## Field by field

### `engagement_id` (required)

Free-form string. Every artifact envelope, the register anchor
(`<engagement_id>:<seq>:<chain_sha256>`), the private baseline
(`private/baseline.<engagement_id>.json`) and `purge --engagement <id>` are
keyed by it. Pick something stable for the life of the engagement; start a
new id for a new engagement rather than editing an old one.

### `slug` (required, `^[a-z0-9-]+$`)

Lower-case, digits and hyphens. Used in paths the bundle publishes to:
`tasks/security-<slug>-admitted/` (the hand-off suite) and
`.agents/security-testing/handoffs/<slug>.md`.

### `scope_paths` and `product_paths` (required, may be `[]`)

Repo-relative path prefixes (`src/`, `services/api/`) or files
(`package.json`). Both lists are unions of prefixes; a path is "under" the
list when it starts with one of them.

- `scope_paths` — what the reviewers look at. `scope` enumerates tracked
  files under these prefixes (plus anything you `--include`) into
  `scope.json`; findings must cite files inside it.
- `product_paths` — what counts as the product. Usually a superset of
  `scope_paths`: build files, lockfiles, config the review does not read but
  a change to which would matter.

Together, `scope_paths ∪ product_paths` are the paths the **assessment
cleanliness check** covers (spec D18, amendment P6). `run init --kind
assessment` and `scope` require `git status --porcelain -- <scope_paths>
<product_paths>` to be empty after excluding the bundle's own managed paths
(`.agents/security-testing/**`, `reports/security/`, `tasks/security-*/`,
and the managed block in the root `.gitignore`); anything else dirty under
the assessed paths exits `3 DIRTY-TREE`. Files the engagement itself creates
— this file, the `knowledge/` templates, the rendered `risk-register.md`,
the `.gitignore` block — never count as dirt. Citations resolve at
`head_oid`, which is why the assessed paths must be clean: a citation into
an uncommitted edit could not be re-validated by anyone else.

The same union is what the **baseline** observes (`engagement init` step 4
and `sign-off`): a per-path HMAC of every tracked file and every
non-ignored untracked file under these paths, plus the count of git-ignored
files per path so the excluded coverage is visible in the sign-off output
(`CHANGES-SINCE-BASELINE:`, `EXCLUDED-COVERAGE:`). Paths outside the union
are not observed at all.

`review` and `verify` runs do not require a clean tree; a dirty in-scope
file in a `review` run is stored as a redacted private snapshot with an HMAC
of the original.

### `targets` (required: `tracker`, `browser`, `repo`)

The destination and authority policy (spec §6.6), separate from source
scope. Every host entry is a **bare hostname** with an optional port
(`^[a-z0-9.-]+(:[0-9]+)?$`): no scheme, no path, no user, lower-case.
`https://github.com` is rejected; `github.com` and
`jira.internal.example:8443` are accepted.

- `tracker` — hosts an imported ticket or PR URL may point at (`ingest
  ticket`, `ingest pr`, `ingest tracker-readback`) and the only hosts a
  `ticketed` register event may name. A URL on any other host is inert
  text.
- `browser` — hosts a QA run report, an audit report or a passive case may
  reference (`ingest qa-run`, `ingest audit`, `plan admit`); an observation
  whose environment is elsewhere is recorded as `unknown`.
- `repo` — the `owner/name` of this repository as the tracker knows it, used
  when the `tracker` publication profile builds ticket payloads.

### `base_url` (optional)

The full URL (scheme included — this is not a host entry) that admitted
passive cases are written against and that the `handoff` publication profile
prints. Its host should appear in `targets.browser`.

### `execute_project_tests` (optional)

The **operator record** for `verify.mjs all`, and the only place a test
command can come from — argv never comes from a ticket, a PR body, a SARIF
file or any other imported text. Its authorship is unverified: whoever can
edit this file decides what `verify` runs, so treat edits to this section
like edits to CI configuration.

- `argv` — the test command as an argv array, spawned with `shell: false`
  in a detached worktree of `head_oid`, minimal environment (`PATH HOME LANG
  TERM=dumb CI=1 NO_COLOR=1`), output capped at 64 KiB and redacted. The
  first token must be one of `npm npx pnpm yarn node python python3 pytest
  go cargo mvn ./gradlew gradle make dotnet`; no token may contain shell
  metacharacters or be `-e`, `--eval`, `-c`, `exec`. Omit the whole key and
  `verify` records `NO_TEST_SURFACE`.
- `timeout_s` — integer seconds; default `600`. On expiry the whole process
  group gets `SIGTERM`, then `SIGKILL` after 5 s, and the tests are
  `TESTS_INDETERMINATE(timeout)`.
- `install.argv` — run before the tests (dependency installation). After it
  runs, every tracked file is compared to `head_oid`; a tracked change makes
  the tests `TESTS_INDETERMINATE(install-modified-tree)` unless
  `install.allow_tracked_changes` is `true`, in which case the verdict names
  the HMAC of the tested tree (`tested_tree=<hmac>`) instead of
  `same-as-head`. Untracked files the install creates are recorded by count
  and size only.

### `sign_off` (optional)

`require_dispositions` controls which threat dispositions block `sign-off`:

| value | effect at `sign-off` |
|---|---|
| `executed-or-ticketed` | **default** — every `undisposed` or `planned` threat is listed under `DISPOSITIONS:`; the listing is informational and does not fail sign-off. A threat counts as disposed when it is `executed`, `ticketed`, `accepted` or `mitigated`, each relationship validated by `tm-lint check`, not merely present |
| `all` | any `undisposed` or `planned` threat fails sign-off: `4 DISPOSITIONS(<threat ids>)` |
| `none` | dispositions are not evaluated (`DISPOSITIONS: not evaluated`); use this for an engagement without a threat model |

Omitting `sign_off` means `executed-or-ticketed`.

### `artifact_policy` (optional)

Every security artifact is local by default: `engagement init` step 1 writes
a managed block into the root `.gitignore` (between `# security-testing:begin`
and `# security-testing:end`) with one pattern per artifact directory, and
step 2 fails (`4 TRACKED(<path>)` / `4 NOT-IGNORED(<path>)`) if git tracks
or does not ignore any of them. Set a key to `committed` to remove that
directory's pattern from the block — that is how "commit by policy" works.

| key | pattern removed when `committed` |
|---|---|
| `ledger` | `.agents/security-testing/ledger/` |
| `runs` | `.agents/security-testing/runs/` |
| `receipts` | `.agents/security-testing/receipts/` |
| `proposals` | `.agents/security-testing/proposals/` |
| `handoffs` | `.agents/security-testing/handoffs/` |
| `imports` | `.agents/security-testing/imports/` |
| `register` | `.agents/security-testing/register/` |
| `reports` | `reports/security/` |
| `cases` | `tasks/security-*/` |

There is **no `private` key**: `.agents/security-testing/private/` holds the
HMAC keys, citation records, redacted snapshots and the baseline, and can
never be un-ignored by policy. A record that names `artifact_policy.private`
is refused with `2 POLICY-INVALID(private)` before anything is written.
Committing `runs` or `reports` shares redacted artifacts only; original
bytes never leave `private/`.

This file (`engagement.md`), `risk-register.md` and `threat-model.json` are
outside the managed block and are committed by your normal policy.

## After editing

```
node <skills dir>/security-evidence/scripts/evidence.mjs engagement init
node <skills dir>/security-evidence/scripts/evidence.mjs engagement validate
```

(`<skills dir>` is where your host installs skills — `.claude/skills/` on
Claude Code; see the bundle README for the other hosts.)

`init` prints `TEMPLATES:`, `ENGAGEMENT: present`, `IGNORE-BLOCK:`, `KEY:` and
`BASELINE:` lines; `validate` re-checks the block, the ignore block, tracking,
the key and the baseline without writing anything.
